  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Remnants of an atmospheric river will begin to move in by\n   Thanksgiving morning, causing snow levels to rise above passes\n   initially then changing back to wet snow at night. \n\n-  Wet weather with pass-level and higher elevation valley winter\n   impacts later Friday and Friday night. \n\n-  Brief respite from precipitation this weekend though fog and\n   low clouds continue to be possible. A Warm front to bring \n   another round of precipitation Sunday night into Monday.\n\nCloud cover and very light precipitation are moving into north \ncentral Idaho, with a mix of rain and light snow possible along \nthe Idaho/western Montana border this morning. Otherwise, high \npressure will maintain quieter weather across the region, though \npatchy fog and low clouds remain possible. Incoming mid and high \nlevel clouds should keep fog and low stratus less widespread than \nin previous mornings.\n\nA decaying atmospheric river arrives Thursday, bringing the bulk \nof its moisture into north central Idaho and northwest Montana by \nmorning, before spreading eastward through the day. As this \nmoisture plume advances, milder air aloft will push snow levels \nhigher, causing mountain passes to transition from snow to rain. \nMinimal snow accumulations are possible through mid day over \npasses before this transition occurs. Stronger winds aloft will \nalso generate breezy afternoon conditions, with valley gusts of \n25 to 35 mph, and a drying downslope effect across broader \nvalleys, including the Missoula and Flathead valleys as well as \nsouthwest Montana.\n\nBy Friday morning, the Clearwater Mountains and mountains along \nthe Idaho/Montana border could receive 0.75 to 1.50 inches of \nprecipitation, falling mostly as rain below 5,500 feet. An \nadditional 1.00 to 1.50 inches is possible through Saturday \nmorning as snow levels lower, most likely to between 4,500 and \n5,000 feet.\n\nA surge of colder air from the north late Friday night into \nSaturday morning introduces a challenging rain to snow \ntransition, hinging on how quickly colder air arrives before \nprecipitation ends. While northwestern Montana has a higher \nprobability of lower snow levels Saturday morning, it will likely \nmiss the bulk of the moisture. Farther south across the valleys of\nwest-central Montana, including the Missoula, Mission, and \nnorthern Bitterroot valleys, the most likely outcome Saturday \nmorning remains valley rain with snow staying above 4,000 feet, \naffecting mainly mountain passes. However, there is a low \nprobability (15 to 20 percent chance), of a high impact scenario \nwhere heavier precipitation rates collide with falling \ntemperatures, forcing snow levels down to valley floors earlier \nthan expected. While not the most likely outcome, this scenario \nwould create sudden, slick, and slushy travel conditions along \nportions of I-90 and US-93 in western Montana. Travelers should \nmonitor conditions for potential rapid changes Saturday morning \nbefore precipitation tapers off in the afternoon.\n\nA brief ridge of high pressure moves overhead Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning, though Pacific moisture will continue \nstreaming into the region during the day Sunday. An approaching \nwarm front could bring a brief period of snow to lower elevations \nSunday night before transitioning to rain, with periodic wintry \ntravel impacts possible over mountain passes.",
